1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:05,760
At dawn on November 8, 1942, the people of Casablanca woke to a sound they had never heard

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:10,570
before.

3
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:16,440
Some one hundred American ships off the coast of Morocco. And thirty-five thousand

4
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:21,440
soldiers, who had come from the other side of the ocean.

5
00:00:21,870 --> 00:00:25,450
This is not an illustrated story. These are their real pictures.

6
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:32,780
Let's go back two years.

7
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:39,390
In the summer of 1940, France fell to Germany in about six weeks.

8
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,085
The Germans occupied northern France and left the south to a government that collaborated

9
00:00:43,085 --> 00:00:46,470
with them, in the town of Vichy.

10
00:00:46,930 --> 00:00:54,690
And Morocco, a French protectorate since 1912, came under the Vichy government.

11
00:00:55,130 --> 00:01:00,150
Casablanca was the most important port Vichy still held on the Atlantic.

12
00:01:00,870 --> 00:01:07,880
In December 1941, America entered the war, after the attack on Pearl Harbor.

13
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:11,745
Stalin was pressing his allies for a second front in the West, to ease the pressure on the

14
00:01:11,745 --> 00:01:15,230
Soviet Union.

15
00:01:15,590 --> 00:01:19,780
But invading Europe directly was far too early. So Roosevelt and Churchill chose a closer

16
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:23,970
target: North Africa.

17
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:28,875
The plan: landings in Morocco and Algeria at the same moment. The code name of the

18
00:01:28,875 --> 00:01:33,210
operation: Torch.

19
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:39,660
In late October, the Western Task Force sailed from the coast of Virginia, led by General

20
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:44,710
George Patton and Admiral Kent Hewitt.

21
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:49,330
The convoy crossed the ocean in about two weeks, in total secrecy, under the threat of

22
00:01:49,330 --> 00:01:53,160
German submarines.

23
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:58,230
The plan: three landing points encircling Casablanca.

24
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:04,145
Safi in the south. Fedala, known today as Mohammedia. And Mehdia, near Kenitra, which was

25
00:02:04,145 --> 00:02:09,400
then called Port Lyautey.

26
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:13,913
The Americans hoped the French forces would not open fire. Leaflets were handed out in

27
00:02:13,913 --> 00:02:17,677
Arabic and French, carrying a message from the American president to the people of

28
00:02:17,677 --> 00:02:21,440
Morocco.

29
00:02:22,270 --> 00:02:27,310
But the orders in Rabat and Casablanca were clear: defend.

30
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:30,450
And among the defenders, in the ranks of the French army, were thousands of Moroccan

31
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:32,880
soldiers.

32
00:02:33,670 --> 00:02:37,760
At five in the morning, the landing boats began heading for the beaches.

33
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:45,100
In the port of Casablanca lay a giant French warship: the Jean Bart.

34
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:53,750
She was unfinished and could not sail. But one turret of her huge guns was ready.

35
00:02:54,510 --> 00:02:58,600
Next to her, the coastal guns of El Hank, near the lighthouse.

36
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:02,440
On the morning of the 8th, the Jean Bart exchanged fire with the American battleship

37
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:05,420
Massachusetts.

38
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:11,000
American shells hit her and her turret was knocked out. But French sailors secretly

39
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,030
repaired it... and she opened fire again two days later.

40
00:03:16,670 --> 00:03:21,680
She was only silenced for good by aircraft from the carrier Ranger.

41
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:28,720
Meanwhile, French destroyers and submarines sortied from the port to attack the fleet.

42
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:36,560
Historians consider it the largest one-day naval battle the Atlantic saw in that war.

43
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:44,780
Days later, French destroyers lay wrecked on the shore, and ships sunk in the harbor.

44
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:52,010
At Safi, at around four thirty in the morning, two old American destroyers charged

45
00:03:52,010 --> 00:03:57,840
straight into the harbor and put troops ashore on the quay.

46
00:03:58,490 --> 00:04:04,820
After Casablanca, Safi was the only port able to unload medium tanks.

47
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:10,080
By the afternoon of the first day, Safi had fallen. US Navy photos show people from the

48
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:14,740
town helping to unload supplies.

49
00:04:15,430 --> 00:04:18,480
At Mehdia, the fighting was the fiercest.

50
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:23,010
The objective: the Port Lyautey airfield. On the way, a kasbah some five centuries old

51
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:27,140
guarding the mouth of the Sebou river.

52
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:33,265
On November 10, the American destroyer Dallas went up the narrow Sebou river, guided by a

53
00:04:33,265 --> 00:04:38,750
French pilot who knew the river well, René Malevergne.

54
00:04:39,430 --> 00:04:42,420
She forced her way through the mud and under fire, and landed a special force near the

55
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:45,410
airfield.

56
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:48,910
And before noon that day, the airfield fell.

57
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:54,495
On November 10, Casablanca was surrounded. Patton was preparing an all-out assault on the

58
00:04:54,495 --> 00:04:58,680
city the next morning.

59
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:03,780
But shortly before the assault, the ceasefire order came.

60
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:08,210
On November 11, General Noguès, the French Resident-General in Morocco, arrived in Fedala

61
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:11,920
to negotiate.

62
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:16,720
That day also happened to be Patton's fifty-seventh birthday.

63
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:22,335
But the sea was not calm. That same evening, a German submarine sank an American transport

64
00:05:22,335 --> 00:05:27,050
ship off Fedala.

65
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:32,880
In Europe, Hitler responded at once: Germany occupied all of southern France.

66
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:38,235
In the whole of Operation Torch, in Morocco and Algeria, about four hundred and eighty

67
00:05:38,235 --> 00:05:42,990
Allied soldiers were killed, and more than one thousand three hundred on the French side.

68
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:47,340
And a few weeks later... yesterday's enemies became allies.

69
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:53,575
Just two months later, on January 14, 1943, the President of the United States arrived in

70
00:05:53,575 --> 00:05:58,410
Casablanca.

71
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:03,940
It was the first time an American president had traveled by plane while in office.

72
00:06:04,690 --> 00:06:08,915
In the Anfa district, a hotel and the villas around it were surrounded by barbed wire.

73
00:06:08,915 --> 00:06:13,140
Here, Roosevelt and Churchill met for ten days.

74
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:17,990
One question on the table: where to strike after Africa?

75
00:06:18,770 --> 00:06:22,610
The Americans wanted France directly. The British wanted Sicily and Italy first.

76
00:06:22,610 --> 00:06:26,450
Churchill's view prevailed.

77
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,410
Roosevelt went out to review American troops near Casablanca, then in Rabat, seated in a

78
00:06:30,410 --> 00:06:33,820
jeep.

79
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:38,160
Then an image the Allies wanted as a symbol: a handshake between the two rival French

80
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:42,040
generals, de Gaulle and Giraud.

81
00:06:42,650 --> 00:06:47,130
On the last day, January 24, Roosevelt announced the Allies' condition to the press:

82
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:51,610
unconditional surrender.

83
00:06:53,010 --> 00:06:57,900
But on January 22, a meeting took place that was not on the war agenda.

84
00:06:58,410 --> 00:07:03,410
Roosevelt gave a dinner at his villa in honor of the Sultan of Morocco, Sidi Mohammed ben

85
00:07:03,410 --> 00:07:08,410
Youssef... whom the world would later know as King Mohammed V.

86
00:07:08,970 --> 00:07:14,760
With him was his son, Crown Prince Moulay Hassan, who was thirteen years old.

87
00:07:15,490 --> 00:07:18,865
Patton wrote in his diary that there was no wine on the table... only orange juice and

88
00:07:18,865 --> 00:07:22,240
water.

89
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:29,270
At the same table sat Noguès, the representative of France. In a country under French

90
00:07:29,270 --> 00:07:35,360
protectorate, the American president was honoring the Sultan directly.

91
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:40,470
So... what did those days change, in the war and in Morocco?

92
00:07:41,210 --> 00:07:46,935
First, in the war. Torch was the first major American offensive against Germany. And from

93
00:07:46,935 --> 00:07:52,660
the ports of Morocco and Algeria the road opened to Tunisia, then Sicily and Italy.

94
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,630
Second, a strange irony. The French Army of Africa, with its Moroccan soldiers, which

95
00:07:57,630 --> 00:08:02,260
fired on the Americans in November... fought alongside them months later in Tunisia, then

96
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:06,890
in Italy.

97
00:08:07,710 --> 00:08:12,540
Third, in politics. Some historians believe the Anfa dinner gave the Sultan an

98
00:08:12,540 --> 00:08:17,370
international presence, at a moment no one expected.

99
00:08:18,250 --> 00:08:23,040
Elliott Roosevelt, the president's son, reported that his father spoke that night about

100
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:27,830
Morocco's future after the war. But this is his own account, not confirmed by any official

101
00:08:27,830 --> 00:08:32,620
record, and historians still disagree on its weight.

102
00:08:33,530 --> 00:08:38,440
What is certain is the sequence: about a year later, on January 11, 1944, the Proclamation

103
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:43,350
of Independence was submitted.

104
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:49,115
And thirteen years after that dinner, Morocco became independent in 1956, with the man who

105
00:08:49,115 --> 00:08:54,170
had sat at Roosevelt's table on the throne.

106
00:08:54,910 --> 00:09:00,280
The relationship itself did not begin at Anfa. Morocco was among the first countries to

107
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:05,650
recognize the independence of the United States, back in 1777.

108
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:13,220
One last detail: on January 23, while the leaders were at Anfa, a film called "Casablanca"

109
00:09:13,220 --> 00:09:19,940
opened in American cinemas... and the city's name was on everyone's lips.

110
00:09:21,340 --> 00:09:25,380
Three days of fire, and ten days of meetings... put Casablanca on the map of the Second

111
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:29,420
World War.

112
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:33,505
And you, did you know that America's first major offensive against the Axis began from the

113
00:09:33,505 --> 00:09:36,910
shores of Morocco and Algeria? Tell me in the comments.

114
00:09:37,490 --> 00:09:41,600
Full sources, and all the original pictures, are in the video description.

115
00:09:41,910 --> 00:09:48,140
Subscribe to the channel and turn on the bell... so you don't miss the next archive.

